  "notes": "XGBoost multi-horizon (one regressor per horizon). params={'n_estimators': 400, 'max_depth': 5, 'learning_rate': 0.05, 'subsample': 0.8, 'colsample_bytree': 0.8, 'reg_alpha': 0.0, 'reg_lambda': 1.0}, device=cuda, tree_method=hist. Tuned via Optuna n_trials=50, search minimised RMSE@15 on val; refit on (train+val), eval on held-out test. parent=xgb_temp_real_v1.\nRETRAIN OUTCOME (2026-05-01, Phase D Stage 2): test RMSE@15=0.7933 (success threshold <1.0 -> MET; v1 baseline=1.13, v2 30% better). REPLACED v1 as canonical. ANOMALY: test RMSE@15 (0.7933) is *better* than best_val_rmse@15 (0.8655) - counter-intuitive because hyperparams were selected to minimise val. Root cause is not overfitting to test (test was held out; refit was on train+val with no test signal), but the 70/15/15 chronological split landed val on a more turbulent temperature window than test (per Distribution shift analysis: real temp_c train std=0.47, val std=1.27, test std=0.99). The val partition is genuinely harder than test for this dataset; a model that handles val also handles the (less variable) test cleanly. The reported 0.7933 is honest - it would not survive a different chronological split if the noisy window fell on test instead. Worth flagging in thesis as a limitation of single-fold time-based evaluation on short (5-day) telemetry.\ndataset_provenance: rows_total=103202, rows_after_dropna=103201, rows_dropped=1, target_nan_count=1, strategy=drop, threshold=30000",
